["```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cassert>

class OutdatedIndexMapException { /* Define your exception class here */ };

void insertOrAppend(std::vector<int>& vec, int target, int value, const std::unordered_map<int, size_t>& index_map) {
    auto it = vec.begin();
    size_t pos = index_map.find(target) != index_map.end() ? index_map[target] : vec.end() - 1;

    // Check if the index map and vector are consistent
    if (it != ++vec.cbegin() && *it != target) {
        try {
            throw OutdatedIndexMapException();
        } catch (const OutdatedIndexMapException& e) {
            pos = std::find(it, vec.end(), target) - vec.cbegin();
        }
    }

    vec.insert(pos + 1, value);
}
```

This code provides an efficient implementation of the task using a linear search for quick lookup, while handling potential discrepancies between the index map and vector by checking if the iterators returned from the index map and vector are consistent. If they're not, it performs a linear search on the vector to find the actual position of `target`. The function uses constant memory space complexity (O(1)) for lookup but has a time complexity of O(n) for handling outdated index maps due to linear search."]

