[' Here is a high-level design for your Java function using a custom `TemporalEvent` class, an efficient data structure (a tree-based priority queue), monitoring with a logging service, and dynamic adjustment to varying event frequencies and time windows.

```java
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.NavigableMap;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentSkipListMap;
import com.google.logging.Logger;

public class EventProcessor {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = Logger.getLogger(EventProcessor.class.getName());
    private final NavigableMap<Instant, TemporalEvent> eventQueue;
    private final int timeWindowMilliseconds;
    private PriorityQueue<TemporalEvent> window;

    public EventProcessor(int timeWindowMilliseconds) {
        this.eventQueue = new ConcurrentSkipListMap<>();
        this.timeWindowMilliseconds = timeWindowMilliseconds;
        this.window = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingLong(TemporalEvent::getTimestamp));
    }

    public void processLogEntry(TemporalEvent event) {
        long timestamp = event.getTimestamp();
        Instant instant = Instant.ofEpochMilli(timestamp);

        eventQueue.put(instant, event);

        while (!window.isEmpty() && windowPeek().getTimestamp() + timeWindowMilliseconds < instant) {
            processEventSequence(window.poll());
        }
    }

    private TemporalEvent windowPeek() {
        return window.peek();
    }

    private void processEventSequence(TemporalEvent event) {
        // Handle the sequence of events within the time window here
        LOGGER.info("Processing event sequence: " + event);
        // Log performance metrics using a logging service, if necessary
    }
}
```

In this solution, we use a `ConcurrentSkipListMap` for efficient lookup and update operations when processing log entries. The priority queue (`PriorityQueue`) is used to store the events within the specified time window, ensuring quick access to sequences as new log entries are processed. To handle varying event frequencies and time windows, adjust the `timeWindowMilliseconds` parameter in the constructor.']

