```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <deque>
#include <mutex>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

struct TX_PACKET {
    int RNTI;
    TX_UE_CONFIGURATION_TAG configTag;
    TRANSMIT_POWER_CONTROL_TAG powerCtrlTag;
};

class TX_PacketManager {
private:
    std::deque<TX_PACKET> packets;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::unordered_map<int, std::atomic<size_t>> rntiToIndex;

public:
    void add(const TX_PACKET& packet) {
        // Lock mutex to ensure thread safety
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        packets.push_back(packet);
        auto it = rntiToIndex.find(packet.RNTI);
        if (it != rntiToIndex.end()) {
            it->second++; // Update the index if found
        } else {
            rntiToIndex[packet.RNTI] = std::atomic<size_t>(packets.size() - 1); // Insert new key-value pair
        }
    }

    TX_PACKET& get(int rnti) {
        auto it = rntiToIndex.find(rnti);
        if (it != rntiToIndex.end()) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            size_t index = it->second;
            TX_PACKET& packet = packets[index];
            return packet;
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("RNTI not found");
        }
    }

    void remove(int rnti) {
        auto it = rntiToIndex.find(rnti);
        if (it != rntiToIndex.end()) {
            // Lock mutex to ensure thread safety
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

            size_t index = it->second;
            TX_PACKET packet = packets[index];
            packets.erase(index);
            rntiToIndex.erase(it);
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("RNTI not found");
        }
    }
};
```

This class manages the dynamic array of `TX_PACKET` structures and provides functions for adding, updating, and removing packets based on their `RNTI`. The STL `std::deque` is used for efficient insertion and deletion of elements at both ends. The `std::unordered_map` is employed to store the mapping between RNTI identifiers and their corresponding indices in the packet array.

The `add()` function locks a mutex before adding a new packet and updating its index in the map. This ensures thread safety by preventing multiple threads from accessing the same resources simultaneously. The external data source can update the packets' metadata using the provided `get()` and `remove()` functions, ensuring proper synchronization and handling of potential data loss during updates.

To scale horizontally, you may consider distributing the data across multiple instances of this class running on different hardware components or containers. Balancing between code simplicity and robustness is essential when integrating this solution with existing infrastructure.']

