For generating random strings and hashing passwords, I utilized the well-known C `rand()` library function and sha256 algorithm from OpenSSL respectively. For better performance, consider using a faster random number generator (RNG) and hardware-accelerated cryptographic libraries if available.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <openssl/sha.h>

typedef struct LTE_UPLINK_NAS_TRANSPORT {
    unsigned long sequence_number;
    time_t timestamp;
    NAS_MESSAGE message;
    NAS_PDU pdu;
    struct LTE_UPLINK_NAS_TRANSPORT *next;
} nas_transport;

nas_transport* initializeNasList(unsigned int size, unsigned int char_set_size, const char* passwd) {
    nas_transport* head = NULL;
    nas_transport* current = NULL;
    pthread_mutex_t list_mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        nas_transport* new_node = (nas_transport*) malloc(sizeof(nas_transport));
        if (!new_node) {
            perror("Memory allocation failed!");
            return NULL;
        }

        new_node->sequence_number = i + 1;
        new_node->timestamp = time(NULL);
        new_node->message.length = rand() % char_set_size + 1;
        new_node->message.data = (char*) calloc(new_node->message.length, sizeof(char));
        if (!new_node->message.data) {
            perror("Memory allocation failed!");
            free(new_node);
            return NULL;
        }

        for (int j = 0; j < new_node->message.length; ++j)
            new_node->message.data[j] = random_char(char_set_size);

        new_node->pdu.length = sizeof(NAS_PDU);
        if (passwd && strlen(passwd)) {
            unsigned char digest[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];
            SHA256((unsigned char*) passwd, strlen(passwd), digest);
            memcpy(new_node->pdu.data, digest, sizeof(digest));
        }

        new_node->next = NULL;

        pthread_mutex_lock(&list_mutex);
        if (!head) {
            head = current = new_node;
        } else {
            current->next = new_node;
            current = new_node;
        }
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&list_mutex);
    }

    return head;
}

char random_char(unsigned int char_set_size) {
    return (rand() % char_set_size + 1) + 'a' - char_set_size;
}
```

This implementation assumes a fixed-size character set for the `NAS_MESSAGE`. You can modify the `random_char()` function to generate random characters from your desired character set. Keep in mind that memory allocation and deallocation will impact overall system performance, especially under high load conditions. Using dynamic memory allocation with appropriate error handling should ensure graceful failure when faced with allocation errors.

Using static vs. dynamic memory allocation depends on the specific use case. For a linked list of varying size like this one, dynamic allocation is more suitable because it allows for flexible sizing without pre-allocating a fixed amount of memory. However, you may want to consider using preallocated arrays or custom memory pools in cases where predictable performance and minimized memory fragmentation are crucial.

Customizing the hashing process can improve performance and data integrity depending on specific requirements such as security level, throughput, and power consumption. The default hashing algorithm used here is a reasonable choice for general-purpose applications but may not be ideal in all cases. When using OpenSSL for hash computation, ensure you provide a properly initialized context to maximize performance gains.

This implementation handles edge cases such as zero-length strings by not allowing the creation of `NAS_MESSAGE` with a length of 0, and invalid input parameters by returning NULL when memory allocation fails. Testing under various load conditions will help ensure acceptable performance levels while facilitating future maintenance and scaling.']

